# An ad is delivered only when the user can click it

"Committed", "pushed", "rendered locally" are not delivery. Delivery is a
URL the user opens and watches, verified live before you type it. The Pages
viewer (`spot.html`) plays the spot's live source, so even a source-only
edit to `assets/spots/<name>.js` is clickable with no re-render — you never
have a reason to hand back a spot as a path.

Any reply that hands over a spot (new, changed, re-rendered) ENDS with a
`Links` block — the last thing in the reply, no trailing summary after it:

- one viewer link per ratio you touched or rendered:
  `https://singularitystudiosdev.github.io/superbot-ads/spot.html?spot=<name>&ratio=<16:9|1:1|9:16>`
- each mp4 you rendered:
  `https://singularitystudiosdev.github.io/superbot-ads/videos/<name>-<16x9|1x1|9x16>.mp4`
- a dedicated page (`board.html`, `said.html`) only if it exists and you
  changed it — the viewer link already covers every other spot.

Ratio spelling differs by target: colon in the viewer (`9:16`), x in the
mp4 filename (`9x16`). Label each link in 2–3 words (`live capture`,
`16:9 mp4`) and use full URLs with scheme — repo-relative paths and
`assets/…` file paths don't count; the user is not in your checkout.

Verify before you link: `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' <url>` must
print 200. A Pages deploy lags the push by a minute or two — re-run the curl
until 200 or ~2 minutes; if it still isn't live, say "deploy still landing"
rather than presenting a 404 as a view link.

Bad:  "board is committed and pushed ✅"
Good: "view it: [live capture](…/spot.html?spot=board&ratio=16:9) ·
      [16:9 mp4](…/videos/board-16x9.mp4) · [9:16 mp4](…/videos/board-9x16.mp4)"
